if ARCH_CHIP_CUSTOM

comment "BK7258 Configuration Options"

choice
	prompt "Beken Chip Selection"
	default ARCH_CHIP_BK7258

config ARCH_CHIP_BK7258
	bool "bk7258"
	select ARCH_ARMV8M
	select ARCH_CORTEXM33
	select ARCH_HAVE_FPU
	select ARCH_HAVE_MPU
	select ARCH_HAVE_FETCHADD
	select ARCH_HAVE_IRQPRIO
	select ARM_HAVE_MPU_UNIFIED
	select ARMV8M_HAVE_STACKCHECK
	select ARMV8M_USEBASEPRI
	select ARCH_HAVE_LEDS
	select ARCH_HAVE_BUTTONS
	select ARCH_HAVE_IRQBUTTONS
	select ARCH_HAVE_SDIO
	---help---
		Beken BK7258: Armv8-M STAR-MC1 (Cortex-M33 compatible) MCU up to
		480MHz, with Wi-Fi 6 and Bluetooth LE 5.4, 640KB shared SRAM,
		XIP flash and SiP PSRAM.

endchoice

menu "BK7258 Peripheral Support"

config BK7258_UART0
	bool "UART0"
	default y
	select UART0_SERIALDRIVER
	select ARCH_HAVE_SERIAL_TERMIOS
	---help---
		UART0 is routed to GPIO10 (RX) / GPIO11 (TX), which is the same
		pin pair used by the ROM flash-download engine (DL_UART).  On the
		Agora ConvoAI Kit R1 these pins are wired to the on-board CH340
		USB-serial bridge, so UART0 is the natural console.

config BK7258_UART1
	bool "UART1"
	default n
	select UART1_SERIALDRIVER
	select ARCH_HAVE_SERIAL_TERMIOS
	---help---
		UART1 on GPIO0 (TX) / GPIO1 (RX).

config BK7258_UART2
	bool "UART2"
	default n
	select UART2_SERIALDRIVER
	select ARCH_HAVE_SERIAL_TERMIOS
	---help---
		UART2 on GPIO40 (RX) / GPIO41 (TX), alternate GPIO30/GPIO31.

config BK7258_WIFI
	bool "WiFi network device"
	default n
	select NETDEVICES
	select NETDEV_LATEINIT
	select NETDEV_WIRELESS_HANDLER
	select NETDEV_IOCTL
	select NET_ETHERNET
	select DRIVERS_IEEE80211
	select WIRELESS
	---help---
		Register wlan0 as an 802.11 netdev lower half with wireless
		extensions, per the openvela network driver guide.

		**This cannot carry traffic yet.**  Bring-up currently reaches
		mm_init() inside the closed MAC and faults there; the association
		parameters and the wireless ioctls are real, but there is no RX or
		TX path behind them yet.

		On its own this registers wlan0 and nothing more.  Turn on
		BK7258_WIFI_VENDOR as well to build and drive Beken's MAC.

		See PORTING_NOTES chapter 21.

config BK7258_WIFI_VENDOR
	bool "Compile Beken's WiFi driver sources"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_WIFI
	---help---
		Build components/bk_wifi/src from the Beken SDK into this image.

		The build flags come from the vendor's own build rather than from
		guesswork -- see chip/bk7258_wifi_vendor.cmake for how to
		regenerate them.  Two of them are not optional: without
		-DBK_MAC=1 the version macro that gates half the driver is never
		defined, and the 113 include directories have to keep their order
		so that same-named headers resolve to the bk7258 copy.

		Requires the Beken SDK checked out; BK_IDK_ROOT points at it.

config BK7258_WIFI_ATE
	bool "Answer yes to Beken's ATE (factory test) probe"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_WIFI_VENDOR
	---help---
		Make ate_is_enabled() report that the chip is in Beken's factory
		test mode.  It is not -- that mode is entered by a strapping pin
		their boot loader samples -- so this is a lie told deliberately,
		and the product configuration should leave it off.

		It exists because of one of the four places the stack asks.  With
		the honest answer, rwnx_intf_init() ends wifi_init() by calling
		rwnxl_sleep() and ps_env_set_ps_on(true): the MAC is parked in
		doze with power save on, and this port has no wake machinery
		outside the WiFi core thread.  Anything that reaches the MAC from
		another context then finds it asleep, which is what the vendor's
		own assert says -- "MAC is in doze, open maccore and phy clock",
		at nxmac_current_state_getf:2001.  Beken's radio tools never meet
		this because they run with ATE on.

		The other three sites are harmless and, if anything, helpful:
		rw_ieee80211_init sets a per-channel regulatory byte,
		rwnx_handle_dynparams (rwnx_params.c:775) enables VHT/HE MCS 0-9,
		and the fourth is the adapter slot itself.  None of them expects
		test equipment on the other end of the radio, contrary to what the
		comment in bk7258_wifi_shim.c used to claim.

		bk7258_wifi_ate_enable() overrides this at run time; it has to be
		called before bk7258_wifi_ifup(), which is where bk_wifi_init()
		runs.

config BK7258_WIFI_WPA
	bool "WPA2/WPA3 via the vendor wpa_supplicant"
	default n
	depends on BK7258_WIFI_VENDOR
	---help---
		Compile Beken's wpa_supplicant 2.10 (open source, in the SDK) and
		the mbedtls crypto subset it needs into the vendor object code,
		enabling WPA2-PSK and WPA3-SAE association.  Without it the port
		associates open-system only.

		This is the vendor's own STA configuration: the same 78 supplicant
		files its sta_connect build compiles (crypto backed by mbedtls, as
		CONFIG_USE_MBEDTLS in the vendor sdkconfig dictates -- the
		internal-crypto alternative preprocesses to empty objects under
		that header and cannot be chosen from outside it).

		Turning this on retires the shim's wpa_ctrl_*/sk_intf stubs and the
		glue's own get_ie/get_security_type_from_ie -- the real supplicant
		defines all of them -- and the received-EAPOL fork in
		ethernetif_input becomes live so the 4-way handshake can happen.

		One deliberate substitution: the supplicant's os_get_random() and
		mbedtls's entropy source are rand()-backed in the vendor tree; here
		both are rebound to arc4random_buf(), the CSPRNG this image already
		carries.  SAE and the WPA2 nonces are only as good as this source.

config BK7258_FLASH
	bool "On-chip flash MTD"
	default n
	---help---
		The flash controller at 0x44030000, exposing the part of the 8MB
		SPI flash that no partition claims as an MTD device.

		The firmware executes in place out of this same chip, so the region
		is not negotiable: the vendor partition table ends at 0x400000 and
		everything below it belongs to the boot loader, the three cores'
		app images, and the RF calibration data that the BLE stack reads.
		Only the space above that is offered, and the driver refuses any
		address outside it rather than trusting its caller.

		Note the two address spaces.  The controller inserts two CRC bytes
		every 32 on the CPU's instruction path, so an XIP address is 32/34
		of a physical one; the software register path used here is raw
		physical bytes and no CRC is involved.  src/flashtest.c dumps the
		controller state and known regions if that ever needs re-checking.

if BK7258_FLASH

config BK7258_FLASH_OFFSET
	hex "Region start (physical byte offset)"
	default 0x500000
	---help---
		Physical offset of the first sector the MTD may touch.  Must be
		sector aligned.

		The default is measured, not chosen for roundness.  The vendor
		partition table fills exactly 0x400000 and stops; a scan of the
		space above it found 0x400000..0x4d0000 already carrying
		CRC-encoded erased content from the factory tooling, and nothing
		between 0x4d0000 and 0x7fa000.  0x500000 clears the written part
		with margin.

config BK7258_FLASH_SIZE
	hex "Region size in bytes"
	default 0x2f0000
	---help---
		Length of the region, sector aligned.  The default ends at
		0x7f0000, leaving the last 64KB alone: the top six sectors are in
		use, and the one at 0x7fe000 begins with "TLV", which is factory
		data this board's RF calibration is very likely to need.  Erasing
		it is not something a test should be able to do by accident.

config BK7258_FLASH_BLOCKSIZE
	int "MTD read/write block size"
	default 512
	---help---
		Must divide the 4096-byte erase sector and be a multiple of the
		controller's 32-byte transfer unit.  512 is the default because
		FAT expects sectors of at least that size.

endif # BK7258_FLASH

config BK7258_TIMER
	bool "Oneshot timer (TIMER group 0)"
	default n
	select ONESHOT
	select ONESHOT_COUNT
	---help---
		The timer group at 0x44810000, published as /dev/oneshot0.  Two of
		its three channels are used: one is armed per request, the other
		free runs as the timebase that ONESHOT_CURRENT reports.  Both sit
		behind interrupt 3.

		This does not touch the system tick.  The scheduler runs off the
		AON RTC through arch_alarm(), and /dev/rtc0 owns that block's
		alarm, so a test can hammer the oneshot without perturbing either.

		The driver implements the count-based half of the oneshot
		interface, hence the ONESHOT_COUNT select -- the two halves are
		different function signatures behind the same struct, so the
		symbol is a build requirement rather than a preference.

config BK7258_RTC
	bool "AON RTC"
	default n
	select RTC
	select RTC_ARCH
	select RTC_DRIVER
	select RTC_EXTERNAL
	select ONESHOT_COUNT if ALARM_ARCH
	---help---
		The always-on RTC counter at 0x44000200, used as the system time
		source and published as /dev/rtc0.

		RTC_EXTERNAL is selected even though this RTC is on-chip.  What
		that symbol actually controls is *when* the OS expects a readable
		clock: with it clear, clock_initialize() reads the RTC during
		nx_start(), which is far too early here -- an AON access issued
		before the domain is ready stalls the bus on this SoC, and the
		driver's clock-rate measurement needs a running system tick
		besides.  With it set, the OS waits and the clock is synchronised
		from board_late_initialize() instead.

		RTC_HIRES is deliberately not selected.  It would require
		CONFIG_RTC_FREQUENCY as a compile-time constant, but the AON
		counter runs from either the 32768 Hz crystal or the 32000 Hz
		internal oscillator depending on what the bootloader selected, so
		the rate is measured at init.  Naming one of them in a config
		symbol would state something the driver cannot promise.

config BK7258_WDT_NMI
	bool "Watchdog NMI stage (stack dump before the reset)"
	default n
	---help---
		Run the peripheral-domain watchdog at 0x44800000 alongside the
		always-on one, with a shorter period so it bites first.  It raises
		the NMI exception rather than resetting, which buys a stack dump and
		-- more usefully -- a chance to record the bite in the always-on PMU
		field, so the next boot reports BOARDIOC_RESETCAUSE_SYS_RWDT instead
		of "power on".  The always-on watchdog still does the actual reset.

		NMI is outside PRIMASK and BASEPRI on Cortex-M, so this stage lands
		even from inside a critical section, which is what the xTS watchdog
		case (1.3.15) requires of a driver.

		Turning this off restores exactly the previous behaviour: the
		always-on watchdog resets the chip with no dump and no recorded
		reason.

config BK7258_WDT
	bool "Watchdog character driver (/dev/watchdog0)"
	default n
	select WATCHDOG
	---help---
		Publish the AON watchdog as /dev/watchdog0.

		The SysTick heartbeat already re-arms this watchdog as the system's
		lockup net, and that keeps working whether or not this is enabled.
		What the driver adds is a deadline the heartbeat honours: once an
		application starts /dev/watchdog0 and then stops pinging it, the
		heartbeat arms the 6 ms boot period and the SoC resets promptly,
		rather than waiting out the ~65 s running period.

config BK7258_I2C1
	bool "I2C1 (GPIO0 SCL / GPIO1 SDA)"
	default n
	select I2C
	---help---
		Polled I2C master on the I2C1 block.  On the Agora ConvoAI Kit R1
		this bus reaches the SC7A20 accelerometer; the pins double as the
		UART-expansion header pads.

config BK7258_SPI1
	bool "SPI1 (GPIO2 SCK / GPIO3 CSN / GPIO4 MOSI)"
	default n
	select SPI
	---help---
		Polled transmit-only SPI master on the SPI1 block.  These pins are
		screen 1 of the twin GC9D01 panels on the Agora ConvoAI Kit R1;
		GPIO5, nominally MISO, is the panel's D/C line.

endmenu # BK7258 Peripheral Support

config BK7258_EARLY_MARKERS
	bool "Emit early bring-up markers on UART0"
	default n
	---help---
		Push single characters straight into the UART0 TX FIFO at each step
		of __start(), before this port configures the clocks, the pins or
		the serial driver.

		These only work once bk7258_lowsetup() has configured the console.
		The Beken bootloader does not leave UART0 running: it deinitialises
		the UART and touches the system clock registers immediately before
		branching to the application, so markers placed ahead of the port's
		own UART setup write into a peripheral that has been torn down.

		Markers: 1=entry, 2=VTOR set, 3=FPU on, 4=.data/.bss ready,
		5=clocks up, 6=console configured.

config BK7258_LED_PROBE
	bool "Blink the board LEDs at __start()"
	default n
	---help---
		Alternate the two board LEDs (red on GPIO40, green on GPIO41 per the
		schematic) forever, as the first thing __start() does.

		This answers "did the bootloader hand control to this image?" using
		only the always-on GPIO block: no clock gating, no pin mux, no UART,
		and no assumption about whether SYSRESETREQ reboots this SoC.  If the
		LEDs alternate, the image is running.

		The board will not boot anything while this is enabled.

config BK7258_TXPIN_PROBE
	bool "Bit-bang GPIO11 (UART0 TX) at __start()"
	default n
	---help---
	  Toggle GPIO11 as a plain GPIO, roughly a millisecond per level, as the
	  first thing __start() does.

	  GPIO11 is UART0 TX and reaches the CH340 bridge, confirmed by both the
	  datasheet and the board schematic.  Every level change lands in the
	  host's receiver as a framing error, so the host sees a steady stream of
	  bytes: reading anything at all from the port proves this image executes.

	  Unlike the LED probe this needs no clock gate, no pin mux, no UART
	  peripheral, no power rail, and nothing for a person to watch -- the
	  answer comes back over the same cable used for flashing.

	  The board will not boot anything while this is enabled.

config BK7258_TXPIN_TRACE
	bool "Bit-bang a GPIO11 burst at each step of __start()"
	default n
	---help---
	  Emit one burst on GPIO11 at each step of the boot -- entry, vector table
	  and FPU, .bss/.data, clocks, console, board init -- with the line held
	  idle in between so the host sees them as separate runs of bytes.

	  Counting the bursts locates the step that hangs without needing a
	  working console, a debugger, or one flash cycle per checkpoint.  Six
	  bursts means __start() ran to completion and nx_start() was entered.

	  Unlike the other probes this one does not stop the boot: it only slows
	  it down, about half a second per step.

config BK7258_UART_DUMP
	bool "Shift the console UART's registers out on GPIO11"
	default n
	---help---
	  After bk7258_lowsetup() returns, read the console UART's line
	  configuration, FIFO status and device id back and shift each out on
	  GPIO11 as a 32-bit pattern -- long burst for a one, short for a zero --
	  repeating forever.

	  Use this when the console produces nothing and there is no way to ask it
	  why: arm_lowputc() waits on write-ready with no timeout, so a UART that
	  never asserts it stops the boot silently.  A device id of zero says the
	  block has no clock; a plausible id with write-ready clear says it is
	  clocked but misconfigured.

	  The board will not boot anything while this is enabled.

config BK7258_TXPIN_PROBE_LATE
	bool "Bit-bang GPIO11 just before nx_start()"
	default n
	---help---
	  The same GPIO11 bit-bang as BK7258_TXPIN_PROBE, but placed after the
	  clocks, the console and board_early_initialize() rather than before
	  them, so the two together bisect the boot.

	  Traffic on the port means everything this chip layer does completed and
	  a fault would be inside NuttX; silence means the boot dies somewhere in
	  bk7258_start.c above the call.

	  The board will not boot anything while this is enabled.

config BK7258_SELFRESET_PROBE
	bool "Self-reset probe at __start()"
	default n
	---help---
		Spin for about half a second at the very top of __start() and then
		trigger a system reset, forever.

		This answers "did the bootloader hand control to this image?"
		without relying on the console, which is useful precisely when the
		console is the thing that is not working.  If this code runs, the
		board cycles through the bootloader roughly twice a second, so its
		UART download window keeps reopening and tools/bk_flash.py links up
		with no manual RST press at all.  If a manual reset is still
		needed, the image never got control.

		The reset is done by arming the watchdog, not by writing ARMv8-M's
		SYSRESETREQ: the vendor SDK never touches AIRCR anywhere, and both
		its bk_reboot() and the Beken bootloader reboot through the
		watchdog, so SYSRESETREQ does not appear to reset this SoC.  No pin
		is driven, so this cannot contend with anything else on the board.

		The board will not boot anything while this is enabled.  Turn it
		off once the question is answered.

endif # ARCH_CHIP_CUSTOM

config BK7258_BATTERY
	bool "Battery gauge (/dev/batt0)"
	default n
	select BATTERY_GAUGE
	---help---
		Cell voltage, charge percentage and charger state for the
		ConvoAI Kit R1.  Voltage comes from SARADC channel 0, the
		charger's two status pins are GPIO51 and GPIO26, and every
		constant is taken from the vendor's own battery monitor
		(bk_avdk_smp bk_batt_monitor/bat_monitor.c).

		Charging itself needs no software: the ETA4322 on this board
		runs on its own and exposes no control interface.  This driver
		only reports.

config BK7258_HCI_TRANSPORT
	bool "H4 HCI transport character device"
	default n
	---help---
		Register /dev/ttyHCI0, an H4 HCI byte pipe onto the closed BLE
		controller, so the openvela Bluetooth service (and therefore
		bttool and the xTS bluetooth cases) can drive this radio the
		way it drives any other.  The node only carries traffic; the
		controller is brought up separately.
